        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why do perfumes behave differently in the UAE heat?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Warm skin pushes scent molecules off faster, so fragrances blast off quickly then fade. Humidity carries scent further but also breaks down lighter notes like citrus and florals fast, while heavier woods, ambers and resins open up and become richer in the heat."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What does a fragrance wardrobe mean?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A fragrance wardrobe means owning a small rotation of scents, each picked for a different moment, rather than relying on one signature bottle. Choices split by occasion, season, and time of day, with most people needing just four or five scents to cover their week."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why are young professionals in Dubai choosing niche perfumes?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Gen Z shoppers grew tired of smelling like everyone else wearing the same mall fragrances, so they looked past familiar names. Niche houses build scents around a mood or idea rather than chasing mass appeal, giving buyers more personality and individual identity."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why do long-lasting fragrances underperform in the Gulf?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Fragrances marketed as long lasting are often tested and tuned for temperate weather, not 40-degree heat. Once they land in Gulf conditions, the same formulas fade faster or behave unpredictably because they were never built with this climate in mind."
          }
        }
      ]
    }
